// ============================================================================
// Detection: Container Escape via nsenter
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1611 - Escape to Host
// Source: AKSAudit + ContainerLogV2
// ============================================================================

// HOW THIS DETECTION WORKS
// -------------------------
// `nsenter` enters another process's Linux namespaces. Inside a privileged
// container an attacker runs `nsenter --target 1 --mount ...` to jump into the
// HOST's namespaces (PID 1 = the node's init) and effectively break out of the
// container onto the node — that is T1611 (Escape to Host).
//
// This query is LOG/STDOUT-based: it scans container stdout/stderr captured in
// Log Analytics for the literal string "nsenter". It is the cheap, always-on
// complement to the Falco syscall rule (detections/falco/container-escape.yaml),
// which catches the actual process spawn at the kernel level.
//
// TABLE: ContainerLogV2 — the Azure Monitor for Containers table holding one row
//        per container stdout/stderr line, enriched with pod/namespace metadata.

ContainerLogV2                       // read collected container console logs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)      // time filter first (cheapest, prunes the scan window)
| where LogMessage has "nsenter"     // `has` = whole-token match on the log text; the escape tell
| project TimeGenerated, PodName, PodNamespace, ContainerName, LogMessage  // keep only the analyst-relevant columns
| extend MitreTechnique = "T1611"    // tag every hit with the ATT&CK technique for triage/reporting
